<% content_for :title, "Shinto & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .shinto-ifs-page .ifs-copy,
  .shinto-ifs-page .ifs-copy > div {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .shinto-ifs-page .ifs-source {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.75em;
    font-style: normal;
  }

  .shinto-ifs-page .ifs-bipolar-hero {
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        rgba(8, 8, 8, .9) 0%,
        rgba(8, 8, 8, .62) 42%,
        rgba(8, 8, 8, .2) 100%
      ),
      url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_shinto_desktop.webp') %>");
    background-position: center right;
    background-size: cover;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .shinto-ifs-page .ifs-bipolar-hero {
      background-image:
        linear-gradient(
          180deg,
          rgba(8, 8, 8, .3) 0%,
          rgba(8, 8, 8, .56) 48%,
          rgba(8, 8, 8, .94) 100%
        ),
        url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_shinto_mobile.webp') %>");
      background-position: center, center bottom;
    }
  }
</style>

<main class="ifs-bipolar-page shinto-ifs-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="ifs-bipolar-hero">
    <div class="ifs-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="ifs-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / SHINTO
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>SHINTO LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="ifs-bipolar-intro">
        THE WORLD KEEPS TURNING.<br>
        YOU CAN KEEP BEGINNING AGAIN.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE SHINTO PERSPECTIVE -->
  <section class="ifs-section ifs-pink"
           aria-labelledby="shinto-section-1-title">

    <div class="ifs-section-heading">
      <p class="ifs-number">01 / THE SHINTO PERSPECTIVE</p>

      <h2 id="shinto-section-1-title">
        STAY<br>
        CONNECTED.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ifs-copy">

      <blockquote>
        <p>"Even the wishes of an ant reach to heaven."</p>
        <cite class="ifs-source">—Traditional Shinto proverb</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Shinto emphasizes relationships with nature, the kami, and the
        people and places around you. Ritual, gratitude, and participation
        in everyday life help maintain those connections.
      </p>

      <p>
        Living with bipolar disorder doesn't mean achieving a permanently
        balanced mind. It means continuing to care for your relationships
        and your life, even when your internal experience changes.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        YOUR MOOD CAN CHANGE.<br>
        YOUR CONNECTION TO LIFE CAN REMAIN.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / KEGARE -->
  <section class="ifs-section ifs-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="shinto-section-2-title">

    <div class="ifs-section-heading">
      <p class="ifs-number">02 / KEGARE</p>

      <h2 id="shinto-section-2-title">
        YOU ARE<br>
        NOT<br>
        UNCLEAN.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ifs-copy">

      <p>
        In Shinto, <em>kegare</em> refers to impurity or disruption.
        It shouldn't be used to describe bipolar disorder as spiritual
        dirt, punishment, or moral failure.
      </p>

      <p>
        Depression can leave you withdrawn and disconnected. Mania can
        overwhelm your life with racing thoughts, spending, plans, and
        impulses. Afterward, you may be left dealing with consequences
        and regret.
      </p>

      <p>
        These experiences can disrupt your life without defining your
        character. They deserve treatment, attention, and sometimes repair.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        DISRUPTION DOESN'T MEAN<br>
        THERE'S SOMETHING WRONG WITH YOUR WORTH.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / PURIFICATION AND RENEWAL -->
  <section class="ifs-section ifs-black"
           aria-labelledby="shinto-section-3-title">

    <div class="ifs-section-heading">
      <p class="ifs-number">03 / HARAE AND MISOGI</p>

      <h2 id="shinto-section-3-title">
        BEGIN<br>
        AGAIN.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ifs-copy">

      <p>
        Shinto purification practices such as <em>harae</em> and
        <em>misogi</em> emphasize renewal. They aren't psychiatric
        treatments, and they can't erase an episode or its consequences.
      </p>

      <p>
        Renewal can still be meaningful. Washing your face, taking a
        shower, changing clothes, or cleaning a small space can become
        simple ways to return your attention to the present.
      </p>

      <p>
        After a difficult episode, you can acknowledge what happened,
        apologize where necessary, repair what you can, and move forward
        without pretending the past never occurred.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        YOU CAN'T ERASE THE PAST.<br>
        YOU CAN BEGIN AGAIN.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / THE KAMI AND NATURE -->
  <section class="ifs-section ifs-purple"
           aria-labelledby="shinto-section-4-title">

    <div class="ifs-section-heading">
      <p class="ifs-number">04 / THE KAMI AND NATURE</p>

      <h2 id="shinto-section-4-title">
        THE WORLD<br>
        IS BIGGER<br>
        THAN YOUR<br>
        MOOD.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ifs-copy">

      <p>
        Shinto recognizes the kami, sacred presences associated with
        nature, places, ancestors, and other dimensions of existence.
        Nature can be a source of reverence and connection.
      </p>

      <p>
        A mountain can still matter when you're depressed. Trees keep
        growing while your thoughts race. The seasons continue whether
        or not you feel ready for change.
      </p>

      <p>
        Even when pain or exhaustion keeps you inside, you can notice
        the sky, weather, or a plant near your window. Your immediate
        mental state isn't the entirety of reality.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        YOUR WORLD IS MORE<br>
        THAN WHAT YOUR MIND IS DOING.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / RITUAL AND GRATITUDE -->
  <section class="ifs-section ifs-pink"
           aria-labelledby="shinto-section-5-title">

    <div class="ifs-section-heading">
      <p class="ifs-number">05 / RITUAL AND GRATITUDE</p>

      <h2 id="shinto-section-5-title">
        THE SMALL<br>
        THINGS<br>
        STILL<br>
        MATTER.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ifs-copy">

      <p>
        Ordinary routines can offer continuity when your mind feels
        unpredictable. Preparing an offering, tending your altar, or
        caring for your surroundings can help you stay connected to
        familiar parts of life.
      </p>

      <p>
        These practices don't need to be elaborate. During depression,
        something small may be enough. When your mind is racing, a
        familiar routine can provide structure without treating every
        thought as a spiritual message.
      </p>

      <p>
        Gratitude doesn't mean being thankful for bipolar disorder.
        You don't have to appreciate depression, side effects, or the
        consequences of mania.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can appreciate ordinary things: food, shelter, your dog,
        a familiar place, someone who supports you, or a moment of quiet.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        YOU DON'T HAVE TO LOVE YOUR ILLNESS<br>
        TO APPRECIATE YOUR LIFE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / RESTORING HARMONY -->
  <section class="ifs-section ifs-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="shinto-section-6-title">

    <div class="ifs-section-heading">
      <p class="ifs-number">06 / RESTORING HARMONY</p>

      <h2 id="shinto-section-6-title">
        KEEP<br>
        COMING<br>
        BACK.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ifs-copy">

      <p>
        Harmony isn't a permanent state you either achieve or lose.
        It's something you maintain through your relationships,
        responsibilities, surroundings, and care for yourself.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes restoring harmony means resting or accepting help.
        Sometimes it means attending an appointment, apologizing,
        or repairing a relationship.
      </p>

      <p>
        Bipolar disorder may disrupt your connection with yourself,
        but it doesn't remove your ability to participate in life.
        You can return to familiar practices after depression and
        rebuild after impulsive decisions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ritual, gratitude, and nature can't guarantee mood stability
        or replace medication and psychiatric care. They can remain
        meaningful parts of your life alongside treatment.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        HARMONY ISN'T NEVER FALLING APART.<br>
        IT'S CONTINUING TO CARE FOR WHAT MATTERS.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="ifs-ending"
           aria-labelledby="shinto-ending-title">

    <div>

      <p class="ifs-ending-small">
        CLOSING
      </p>

      <h2 id="shinto-ending-title">
        BEGIN<br>
        <span>AGAIN.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="ifs-ending-copy">

      <p>
        You don't have to become untouched by suffering. You can
        acknowledge disruption, care for yourself, and restore your
        connection with the world one ordinary act at a time.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "ifs-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>